insert into public.posts (slug, title, topic, date, read_time, excerpt, status, blocks, sort_order)
values
  ('ai-as-infrastructure', 'Why I stopped thinking of AI as a feature and started thinking of it as infrastructure.', 'AI Engineering', '2026', '6 min read', 'Every product now wants an AI feature. Very few are built to treat AI as the underlying layer the rest of the system stands on. This is the shift that changed how I build.', 'Featured', '[{"type":"p","text":"There is a pattern I keep seeing in AI products. A team bolts an LLM onto an existing app, exposes a chat box, and calls it a feature. It demos well, then falls apart in production — slow, inconsistent, expensive, impossible to monitor."},{"type":"p","text":"The teams that actually ship durable AI products treat the model differently. Not as a feature, but as infrastructure. The same way you think about a database or a queue: a dependable layer that other parts of the product are built on top of."},{"type":"h2","text":"Features are visible. Infrastructure is trusted."},{"type":"p","text":"A feature is something a user notices. Infrastructure is something they never have to think about — it simply works. When AI is infrastructure, the product team designs around its strengths and guards against its weaknesses the way they would guard a database."},{"type":"p","text":"That changes every decision downstream: which model you pick, how you prompt, how you evaluate, how you fail, how you measure cost per call, how you observe a single request end to end."},{"type":"h2","text":"The stack beneath the prompt"},{"type":"p","text":"Treating AI as infrastructure means the interesting work lives around the model, not inside the prompt:"},{"type":"ul","items":["RAG pipelines that give the model the right context at the right time, instead of hoping it knows everything.","Evaluation harnesses that score responses before they ever reach a user.","Fallback chains that route to cheaper or faster models when the workload allows it.","Guardrails that constrain tool use, sandbox execution and cost.","Observability that traces every token back to the request that produced it."]},{"type":"quote","text":"The best AI product I have shipped had no visible ''AI''. It just did what the user asked, reliably and within budget."},{"type":"h2","text":"What changes when you make the shift"},{"type":"p","text":"You stop asking ''what can this model do?'' and start asking ''what must be true for this to be reliable at scale?'' That question is answered in code, not demos."},{"type":"p","text":"It is also a business decision. A model is a commodity you rent. The infrastructure you build around it — your data, your workflows, your guarantees — is the durable part. That is where the moat lives."},{"type":"h2","text":"Start with the infrastructure mindset"},{"type":"p","text":"Before you wire a model into your product, write down what reliability means for it: acceptable latency, cost per interaction, failure behaviour, audit trail. Then design the system that enforces those numbers. The feature will take care of itself."}]'::jsonb, 1),
  ('architecture-is-a-product-decision', 'Architecture is a product decision, not a technical one.', 'Software Architecture', '2026', '5 min read', 'Every architectural choice is a bet on what the business will need next. Treat it as a technical problem and you optimise for the wrong thing.', 'undefined', '[{"type":"p","text":"Most architecture debates are framed as engineering debates. Monolith versus microservices. SQL versus NoSQL. REST versus events. And then everyone argues about the technology — while the real disagreement is about what the business will do next."},{"type":"p","text":"I have stopped making architectural decisions from technical criteria alone. I make them from product criteria, and then let engineering constraints refine the choice."},{"type":"h2","text":"Architecture is a forecast"},{"type":"p","text":"A team adopting microservices is forecasting growth and many autonomous teams. A team adopting a monolith is forecasting a small team shipping fast with low coordination cost. A team adopting event-driven design is forecasting that integrations and audit trails will matter."},{"type":"p","text":"None of these are right or wrong in isolation. They are right when they match the actual forecast."},{"type":"h2","text":"The questions that actually matter"},{"type":"p","text":"Before choosing a stack, answer these with the product team:"},{"type":"ul","items":["How fast does a change need to reach production?","How many teams will touch this system, and can they stay independent?","Which parts will scale, and which will never see more than a few requests a day?","Who pays the cost of complexity — and when?","Which failure modes would hurt the business most?"]},{"type":"quote","text":"If the business case doesn''t justify the complexity, the architecture is wrong even if the code is perfect."},{"type":"h2","text":"Complexity is a budget"},{"type":"p","text":"Every abstraction, service and framework is a line item in your complexity budget. You can spend it, but you only have so much. The cheapest architecture is the one that solves today''s problem simply and is boring enough that the next engineer understands it instantly."},{"type":"p","text":"Most of the painful systems I have encountered were not built by bad engineers. They were built by smart people solving yesterday''s problem with tomorrow''s infrastructure, because someone on the team preferred it technically."},{"type":"h2","text":"Decide with the business"},{"type":"p","text":"Bring the product manager into the architecture conversation. Not to pick the database, but to confirm the forecast the architecture depends on. When the technology choice matches the business trajectory, the system feels inevitable. When it does not, every release is a fight."},{"type":"p","text":"Architecture is a product decision. The code is just where the decision lands."}]'::jsonb, 2),
  ('lessons-from-payment-infrastructure', 'What building payment infrastructure taught me about engineering discipline.', 'FinTech', '2026', '7 min read', 'Payments punish the casual engineer. Money moves, ledgers must balance, and a bug is not a bug — it is a reconciliation problem at midnight.', 'undefined', '[{"type":"p","text":"Building payment infrastructure rewires how you think about software. There is no ''close enough''. A transaction is either settled or it is not, and the difference between the two is where trust lives or dies."},{"type":"h2","text":"Money is just state — until it isn''t"},{"type":"p","text":"At the code level, a transfer is a state change: debit one account, credit another. The discipline comes from making sure that state change is atomic, idempotent and recoverable. Everything else is detail."},{"type":"p","text":"Three rules carry most of the weight:"},{"type":"ul","items":["Idempotency keys on every operation, so retries never double-spend.","Ledgers that record intent and outcome separately from balances.","Reconciliation that detects drift between your system and the provider''s."]},{"type":"h2","text":"Design for the retry, not the happy path"},{"type":"p","text":"Networks fail. Providers time out. Users close the app mid-payment. The happy path is a special case; the retry path is the real product."},{"type":"p","text":"Every payment flow needs a defined state machine: initiated, pending, succeeded, failed, refunded. Each transition must be safe to repeat. If a request times out, the next request must not guess — it must check the actual state and move from there."},{"type":"quote","text":"In payments, the question is never ''did it succeed?'' It is ''what happened, and how do I know?''"},{"type":"h2","text":"The provider is a dependency, not a guarantee"},{"type":"p","text":"Your provider''s uptime is not your uptime. Routing, retries and fallbacks are your responsibility. If your wallet or checkout only works when one provider is healthy, you have not built infrastructure — you have built a dependency."},{"type":"p","text":"Multi-provider routing sounds complex. It is less complex than explaining to a merchant why payments were down for six hours."},{"type":"h2","text":"Tests, but the useful kind"},{"type":"p","text":"Unit tests do not protect you from a double-debit. What does:"},{"type":"ul","items":["Property-based tests on balance invariants — total funds in equals total funds out, always.","Simulated provider failures in staging that exercise every retry and rollback path.","Audit logs that let you replay any account''s transaction history."]},{"type":"h2","text":"Discipline is the product"},{"type":"p","text":"FinTech does not reward cleverness; it rewards discipline. The systems that survive are the boring ones — clear state machines, strict invariants, honest logging. That is the discipline that made me a better engineer everywhere else, and it is why I now treat architecture as an exercise in restraint."}]'::jsonb, 3),
  ('systems-that-last', 'Systems that last: a founder''s approach to technical debt.', 'Engineering Leadership', '2026', '5 min read', 'Technical debt is not a code problem. It is a decision problem — and founders make the most expensive version of it.', 'undefined', '[{"type":"p","text":"Every founder ships fast. The difference between a good founder and a short-lived one is knowing which corners can be cut and which ones cut back."},{"type":"h2","text":"Debt is a choice, not an accident"},{"type":"p","text":"Technical debt is what happens when you knowingly choose speed over durability and never schedule the payoff. The debt itself is fine — the mistake is pretending you did not take it."},{"type":"p","text":"Write the debt down. When you ship a shortcut, record what it was, why you took it, and what would need to happen before it becomes a problem. Most ''surprises'' in mature codebases were once known trade-offs nobody wrote down."},{"type":"h2","text":"Defer the reversible, protect the irreversible"},{"type":"ul","items":["Reversible: naming, structure, internal APIs, component boundaries. Refactor freely.","Irreversible: data model, external contracts, security boundaries, deployment assumptions. Take the time up front."]},{"type":"p","text":"A database schema is a contract written in stone. A function signature is a pencil. Treat them accordingly and the debt that builds up will be the kind you can actually repay."},{"type":"quote","text":"The cheapest time to fix an architecture is before the second customer depends on it."},{"type":"h2","text":"Pay interest, not only principal"},{"type":"p","text":"You cannot always pay down debt while you are finding product-market fit. What you can do is stop the interest compounding:"},{"type":"ul","items":["Keep the deploy pipeline green — broken builds are compounding interest.","Automate the boring checks so regressions surface in minutes, not quarters.","Refactor opportunistically in every feature ticket."]},{"type":"h2","text":"The founder''s rule"},{"type":"p","text":"My rule is simple: the codebase must be healthier at the end of a quarter than it was at the start. Not perfect. Just net positive. Small, consistent investments in durability compound exactly like the shortcuts do — but in the right direction."}]'::jsonb, 4)
